Figure 6.3. Initial Stack Setup Before main() <p>Solved Problem</p> <p>Stack Exploration</p> <p>Problem: Write a program to explore the initial stack layout.</p> <p>Solution:</p> <pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

int main(int argc, char *argv[], char *envp[]) {
    printf("Stack Layout Exploration\n");
    printf("========================\n\n");

    // Main parameters
    printf("argc address:
    printf("argv address:
    printf("envp address:

    // Arguments
    printf("Command-line arguments:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; argc; i++) {
        printf("  argv[
               i, &amp;argv[i], argv[i], argv[i]);
    }

    // Environment (first few)
    printf("\nEnvironment variables (first 3):\n");
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; envp[i]; i++) {
        printf("  envp[
               i, &amp;envp[i], envp[i], envp[i]);
    }

    // Stack addresses show layout
    printf("\nStack grows from high to low addresses\n");
    printf("Note how argc (
           &amp;argc, &amp;argv, &amp;envp);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>$ gcc -o stackexplore stackexplore.c
